def analyze_sort_adaptations(data_structure: str) -> str:
    if data_structure == 'linked_list':
        return (
            "Linked list adaptations: Quicksort is challenging because linked lists do not allow direct access to elements, "
            "and partitioning becomes inefficient. Mergesort is more efficient for linked lists, as it can be implemented "
            "using constant extra space and recursion naturally fits with the properties of linked lists. The algorithm "
            "works well with the sequential access nature of linked lists. Overall, Mergesort for linked lists has a time "
            "complexity of O(n log n), which is competitive due to the ease of merging two sorted linked lists."
        )
    elif data_structure == 'array':
        return (
            "Array adaptations: Quicksort is popular for arrays due to its in-place sorting capabilities and average-case "
            "performance of O(n log n). It is more cache-friendly with arrays as it utilizes locality of reference better. "
            "However, the recursive nature can lead to stack overflow for large arrays unless optimizations like tail-call "
            "elimination are used. Mergesort provides stable sorting with a time complexity of O(n log n) but requires extra "
            "space. Nonetheless, merging is simple and efficient in arrays. Timsort, a hybrid stable sorting algorithm derived "
            "from Mergesort and Insertion Sort, is often used in practice due to its real-world efficiency."
        )
    else:
        return "Data structure not recognized. Please specify either 'linked_list' or 'array'."

